When crafting worlds for games, the designer tends to distribute resources and skills in a way that ends up noticeable to the player #todo[src?]. 
This tendency has a decisive effect in the context of visual features; 
nearby objects get more attention from the player and in turn the designer, while distant objects lose importance.
In linear or closed-world games, the designer can deliberately choose where to allocate resources since playable area is limited #todo[src?]. 
Open-world games, especially those relying on procedural generation, greatly complicate such decisions. 
We can no longer focus on discrete parts but have to instead produce a coherent whole. 
To cope with the vast search space, the designer may intentionally impose rules and constraints, forming a _design language_ with basis in available resources, target audience, and the artistic vision #todo[src?]. 